    "product_id": "b28ab551-a35d-4dac-9fb6-7f2da47a720b",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 161810\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1000 AM PDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms expected today\n  and Thursday across our eastern zones.\n\n* Elsewhere, expect breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts through \n  the week with dry conditions and near-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nExpecting isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon across Lincoln and Mohave counties, with chances\nspreading into Thursday morning. While the southern Great Basin \nremains situated under a dry southwesterly flow aloft, the \nsouthern Mojave Desert will experience a south-southeasterly flow,\nwhich will usher in 1.25 to 1.75 inches of PWAT into the region -\nspecifically southeastern Nevada and northwestern Arizona. In \naddition to the moisture, there is a disturbance riding up the \nBaja California that is currently highlighted by the NHC as having\na 10 percent chance of tropical storm formation in the next 48 \nhours. While this disturbance is unlikely to become a cyclone, as \nit pushes northward, the vorticity maxima associated with it will \nlead the development of showers and thunderstorms across southern \nMohave County / the lower Colorado River Valley this afternoon. \nAdditionally, energy riding along the upper-level trough across \nthe western CONUS has closed off over northern California this \nmorning and will begin its meander eastward across Nevada this \nafternoon. The enhanced jet associated with this closed low will \ncombine with daytime heating to facilitate convection across \neastern Lincoln, eastern Clark, and Mohave counties, which is in-\nline with the latest CAM guidance. That said, there is a noticable\nPWAT gradient across the center of our forecast area, which could\nact as a dryline, resulting in convection developing a bit \nfurther west than guidance currently suggests. Will continue to \nkeep an eye on locations along and west of Interstate 15 as the \nday progresses. As a result of ample instability and moisture, \nthunderstorms that form are expected to be efficient rain- makers \nwith frequent lightning, which will elevate the risk of flash \nflooding, particularly across Mohave County and underneath \ntraining storms. Forecast HRRR soundings in south-central Mohave \nCounty show over 1750 J/kg of SBCAPE, 750-1000 J/kg of DCAPE, 1.50\ninches of PWAT, and 500 mb cooling (LIs around -4 degrees C), so \nsevere thunderstorms are likely as well.\n\nThe trough axis associated with the aforementioned closed low will \npush through the forecast area today and Thursday, resulting in \nelevated south-southwesterly winds with gust speeds generally \nbetween 25 and 35 mph. Cannot rule out occasional gusts to 40 mph \nin the higher terrain. Otherwise, persistent troughing over the \nwestern CONUS will result in afternoon high temperatures near-\nnormal for mid-September. Cloud cover heading into overnight \nperiods may result in elevated morning lows, however.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nGusty SE winds have begun across the Las Vegas Valley today. Similar \nto yesterday, a stronger easterly pressure gradient over the area \nmay keep the easterly wind component around through mid afternoon. \nConfidence is low on the exact hour of the wind shift to S-SW, but \ncurrent thinking is that it should occur after 20z, potentially as \nlate as 22z. Expect gusts 20-25 kt while winds are SE, then gusts up \nto 30 kt possible late evening through 03z after the SSW shift \noccurs. Late afternoon and evening thunderstorm activity is expected \nto remain east of the terminal over northwestern Arizona and the \nMormon Mesa corridor, with VFR conditions prevailing. Not currently \nexpecting outflow from storms to impact the Valley, but will monitor \nstorm and outflow evolution closely. FEW-SCT clouds with bases \naround 10kft can be expected this afternoon and tonight, with \ntemperatures remaining under 100 degrees.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Southeast winds are \nincreasing late this morning with gusts exceeding 20 kt in the CRV. \nExpect a shift to the S-SW around 20-22z. Gusts to around 25KT will \nbe common across the region, with stronger gusts to around 30KT in \nthe northern Owens Valley and along the Lower Colorado River Valley \nwhere up-valley winds develop. KDAG is the usual exception with W \nwinds around 10 kt persisting through the day, then expect the WSW \npush around and after 23z with gusts 20-25+ kt into the early \novernight hours. Elsewhere, speeds and gusts diminish after sunset, \nbut hi-res guidance continues to indicate elevated winds and \nisolated gusts for the CRV overnight with the potential for impacts \nfrom outflow boundaries. Thunderstorms are expected this afternoon \nand evening over eastern areas, and especially over northwestern \nArizona, with impacts from lightning and gusty outflow winds \npossible at both KEED and KIFP. FEW-SCT clouds with bases at or \nabove 10kft are expected this afternoon onward for most of the \nregion, with lower ceilings and greater coverage in the vicinity of \nthunderstorms.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat \nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
